ing a common center to one vertex of each of $K_r$, $K_s$, and $K_t$ by internally disjoint paths of lengths $g$, $h$, and $k$, respectively. As a result, $S^{000}_{rst}$ is Schur positive exactly when $r\\ge st-1$, and every graph $S^{100}_{rst}$ and $S^{010}_{rst}$ is Schur positive. When $s=t$, the graph $S^{001}_{rst}$ is Schur positive; when $s>t$, its Schur-positive members fall into four explicit parameter regimes. We also introduce a path-clique bootstrap and use it to prove that every spider $S(a,b,2)$ is Schur positive. Finally, we prove that the spider $S(a,b,2)$ for $a\\ge b\\ge2$ with $3\\nmid b$ is $e$-positive if and only if $(a,b)\\in\\{(6,4),(12,4),(9,7)\\}$, which advances the study of Tom's conjecture concerning the $e$-positivity of spiders $S(a,b,2)$.\n\n36 pages, 2 figures, 1 table\n\narXiv:2608.22184\nCreated: 2026-08-23 Updated: 2026-08-25\n\nThis refines the $e$-positivity of kayak paddle graphs which was obtained by Aliniaeifard, Wang, and van Willigenburg using an expanded version of Gebhard and Sagan's appendable $(e)$-positivity technique.\n\n22 pages, 8 figures\n\narXiv:2408.01385\nCreated: 2024-08-02\n\n#### The spiders $S(4m+2,\\,2m,\\,1)$ are $e$-positivite\nDavion Q. B. Tang, David G. L. Wang, Monica M. Y. Wang\n\nWe establish the $e$-positivity of spider graphs of the form $S(4m+2,\\, 2m,\\, 1)$, which was conjectured by Aliniaeifard, Wang and van Willigenburg. A key to our proof is the $e_I$-expansion formula of the chromatic symmetric function of paths due to Shareshian and Wachs, where the symbol~$I$ indicates integer compositions rather than partitions. Following the strategy of the divide-and-conquer technique, we pick out one or two positive $e_J$-terms for each negative $e_I$-term in an $e$-expression for the spiders, where $J$ are selected to be distinct compositions obtained by rearranging the parts of $I$.\n\narXiv:2405.04915\nCreated: 2024-05-08\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------